PR review contract — enforced or parsed by the bot, applies when the task is a PR review:
- When the task is "review this PR" (or equivalent), your work ends in exactly one of two outcomes, set on the PR itself: Approve or Request changes. That review state is the deliverable of the task; a review that ends without it is unfinished, whatever the chat reply says.
  - The review is published ONLY as `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<N>/reviews -f commit_id=<SHA> -f event=<APPROVE|REQUEST_CHANGES> -f body=…` (for a long body a heredoc: `-F body=@- <<'EOF'`), and `<SHA>` is the literal 40-hex commit you reviewed, written out in the command: `gh pr review --approve | --request-changes` attaches the review to whatever the PR head is at the moment of sending, a minutes-long review may then land on a commit nobody read, and a hook denies that call, a missing `commit_id`, a `commit_id` from `$(…)` or a shell variable, a `commit_id` other than the SHA the bot recorded, and `--input`. When the prompt lists the reviewed SHA for the PR, that is `<SHA>`; otherwise record `gh pr view <N> -R <owner/repo> --json headRefOid` as your very first step and use that value (the hook then checks only the form).
  - Read the code at `<SHA>`, never at the moving head: the diff is `git diff <merge-base>...<SHA>`, a file is `git show <SHA>:<path>`; run tests and mutations in a temporary `git worktree add /tmp/… <SHA>` that you remove at the end. Do not use `gh pr diff` (it returns the current head) and do not `git checkout` in a working clone (it leaves a detached HEAD and stops the bot's sync).
  - 🟢 → event `APPROVE`, even when there are no findings. 🔴 → event `REQUEST_CHANGES`, findings in the review body. Request changes always goes on `<SHA>`. In an epic run an Approve goes on `<SHA>` too. In a manual review an Approve goes out only if `headRefOid` right before publishing still equals `<SHA>`; otherwise set no status and say in the reply which commits arrived unreviewed. If GitHub rejects the POST (e.g. the commit is no longer in the PR), set no status, say why in the reply and do not republish on another commit. A PR that does not implement its task — partial work, only a preparatory step — is 🔴. A bare comment or a chat-only verdict leaves the PR without a review state, and the human has to set it by hand. If GitHub refuses the state because you are the PR author, post a plain comment instead and say so in the chat reply.
  - When the prompt lists cold reviewer findings with ids (collected by the bot, inline ones included), the review body — an approve too — must carry one line per id with a verdict: fixed, open or refuted. Pass the body in the command itself (`-f body="…"`, or a heredoc): the Stop hook does not read a body from a file (`-F body=@file`, `--input`) and blocks the turn until every id is in the body.
  - Your chat reply is a verdict of AT MOST three lines, nothing else: no praise, no recap of what you did, no description of the review process. Its first line starts with 🟢 or 🔴; the bot parses it. Write the state part only after the review POST succeeded.
- Findings go into a PR review comment or an issue comment — never into the chat. If you could not publish them (expired token, network, rate limit), say so plainly in the chat reply; that line is an explicit exception to the three-line cap above — losing the verdict silently is worse than a longer message.
